[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "2608030100"
  :adr/title "kakure appview: SvelteKit + TypeScript twin → ClojureScript (Reagent/re-frame) on jp-go-dds"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-03"
  :adr/repo "gftdcojp/kakure"
  :adr/deciders "owner (instruction: \"svelte じゃなくて reframe, reagent で jp-go-dds を使って cljs で設計実装して\")"
  :adr/supersedes "the appview half of ADR-2607061200 (the SvelteKit/TypeScript wiring pass); its architecture decisions — direct D1, no dispatcher hop, no pod proxy — are unchanged and still authoritative"
  :adr/related "ADR-2607141915 (jp-go-dds), ADR-2607122200 (kotoba-ui single-entry rule), ADR-2607061200 (direct-D1 wiring), ADR-2607191800 (standalone repo cutover)"
  :adr/context
  "kakure.gftd.ai is the incogni.com-class product in this fleet: it finds a subject on data brokers, drafts jurisdiction-appropriate erasure requests (APPI 第35条 / GDPR Art.17 / CCPA), and watches for re-listing. Six lexicons are published and live.

The appview was SvelteKit. Its domain logic lived in svelte/src/lib/server/kakure.ts, a TypeScript re-implementation of clj/src/kakure/core.cljc whose own file header explained the duplication (\"this repo has no cljs/kototama build bridge that can compile core.cljc into JS runnable inside a Cloudflare Worker\") and instructed the reader to \"keep the two in sync by hand when either changes\". That premise was true when written on 2026-07-06 and stopped being true the moment anyone added a shadow-cljs config — which nobody had, so the duplicate persisted.

Two implementations of a legal workflow, kept in sync by human diligence, is a defect with a countdown on it. The rules being duplicated are not incidental: statutory response deadlines, the authorized-agent consent gate, category sensitivity weights, and the exact statute text quoted in a request sent to a third party on a person's behalf.

Meanwhile gftd.ai's apex (gftdcojp/apex) had already established the pattern this repo needed: Reagent/re-frame + shadow-cljs browser build + a shadow-cljs :esm Cloudflare Worker + jp-go-dds, all in one repo, with the design system swapped from kotoba-ui to DADS by owner directive on 2026-07-31."

  :adr/decision
  "The kakure appview is ClojureScript end to end. Both halves compile from src/ with shadow-cljs and both consume clj/src/kakure/core.cljc directly (deps.edn puts clj/src on :paths — consumed, not copied).

1. Worker (:esm -> dist/_worker.js). kakure.edge routes /xrpc/ai.gftd.apps.kakure.* and falls through to env.ASSETS. kakure.d1 issues SQL and returns ClojureScript data. kakure.wire holds every decision as pure .cljc, so the Worker's behaviour is testable on the JVM without a Cloudflare runtime — which the TypeScript handler never was, and never had been.

2. SPA (:browser -> public/assets). Reagent + re-frame. Pure form->payload transforms live in kakure.db so they can be tested; event handlers are one-liners over them. One :xrpc effect is the single place the app knows how to talk to its Worker.

3. UI: jp-go-dds (デジタル庁デザインシステム). kakure.ui is the only namespace that requires the design system, and requires only jp-go-dds.core — the kotoba-uiux single-entry rule with DADS as its target. Colours are var(--hig-*), bridged onto DADS primitives by jp-go-dds.tokens/skin-css rather than re-derived (jp-go-dds.tokens states plainly that the token contract breaks the moment a second app derives its own bridge). Compositions DADS does not ship — a stat tile, an empty state, a consent block — are namespaced kakure-* beside dds-ext-*, never inside dads-*. Light mode only; upstream DADS has no dark palette and none was invented.

DADS is the right register for this product specifically, not merely the fleet default: these screens ask a person to authorise an agent to file a statutory erasure request on their behalf. That is the register of a government form, in a design language Japanese users have already been taught to read as official.

4. Wire shapes are unchanged. Every key comes from the published lexicons in ai-gftd-apps-gftdcojp's 00-contracts, and the live service answers exactly as before.

5. The RECORDLOG_DB binding was dropped. No handler in this repo opened it; it belonged to the /_d1 pod proxy retired in ADR-2607061200. A live handle on another app's database is not kept for symmetry."

  :adr/consequences
  "Running the .cljc in JavaScript for the first time surfaced two latent bugs that the TypeScript twin had been masking — both of which would have been live defects the moment anyone deployed the pod or trusted the shared namespace:

  - kakure.core/deadline-at's :cljs branch returned `now` unchanged. Every erasure request drafted by a JS runtime would have carried a statutory deadline equal to its creation time, i.e. an escalation clock firing immediately. Now real date arithmetic on both runtimes (measured: 45 days for CCPA, against local D1).
  - kakure.core/pseudo-encrypt used `hash`, which returns different numbers in Clojure and ClojureScript for the same string. The Worker and the reference pod would have written different redaction markers for the same field. Now FNV-1a, identical on both — and byte-identical to the markers the retired TypeScript worker already wrote to production D1 (pinned in test/kakure/wire_test.cljc against the JS implementation it replaces).

The UI is deliberate about two claims kakure cannot honestly make. It never says a request was sent: no mailer is wired, the lexicon field is still called `dispatched` but every label around it says 起案/drafted, and the stored row keeps dispatched=0 with its note. And it never fills an empty state with a plausible number: the production broker registry is unseeded, so a scan honestly returns scanned:0 and the page says the registry is empty rather than rendering a reassuring zero-exposure score. The missing session layer is stated on the page, not hidden.

Costs: a JVM/clojure toolchain is now needed to build the appview (it was already needed for the pod). The bundle is a shadow-cljs build rather than a SvelteKit one. Anyone editing the domain rules now changes behaviour in three runtimes at once — which is the point, and is what the test suite is for."

  :adr/verification
  "- clojure -M:test — 26 tests, 127 assertions, 0 failures. New kakure.wire / kakure.db suites plus the pre-existing kakure.core / kakure.server pod suites, which the core.cljc changes did not regress.
- shadow-cljs release app worker — 0 warnings. The ^js hints on the D1 host object are load-bearing: without them :advanced renames .prepare/.bind/.all and the Worker fails at runtime with no compile-time complaint.
- wrangler dev against a real local D1 (migrations applied, three fixture brokers seeded locally and never remotely): setIdentity without acceptedAuthorizedAgent -> 400; requestRemoval without stored consent -> 400; scanBrokers -> requestRemoval -> getStatus/listBrokers/getExposureReport round-trips; background-graph NSIDs -> 404 with the reason; GET on a procedure -> 405; foreign NSID -> UnknownNSID. Direct SQL confirmed the identity table holds only signal:v1: markers and the drafted request carried deadline_at = +45d with dispatched=0.
- Full UI flow driven headlessly (consent -> save profile -> scan -> draft -> dashboard): zero console errors. A first screenshot caught a real defect — the shell and the mounted app each rendered the masthead and colophon, printing both twice — fixed by making the shell own the static chrome.
- kotoba-lang/design-quality deterministic HIG/WCAG audit on the generated shell: 100.00 (gate min 95).
- Deployed to kakure.gftd.ai (version 39661558-2a0e-4cd5-9b29-016406c5d4e5) and probed live: /health reports runtime cljs with the D1 binding present, the consent gate rejects with 400, and listBrokers returns an empty registry — the true state of the production table."

  :adr/open
  "Unchanged by this ADR and still the repo's real gaps, in priority order:
1. No authentication. actor_did comes from an x-actor-did header with a query/body fallback; anyone can name any subject. Top open item.
2. No broker seed. Populating it needs an authoritative source for names, contacts and removal methods — fabricating those for a legal-erasure system would be actively harmful, so this pass did not.
3. No mailer. requestRemoval drafts and stores; nothing sends.
4. Identity matching gap: pseudo-encrypt is one-way, so stored identities cannot be re-derived server-side. scanBrokers takes a request-scoped hint instead. A real fix needs reversible field encryption with a server-held key, or a client-side match step.
5. Background graphs (track_status / monitor_relisting / escalate / bmc_validation) have no lexicon, no cron, no inbound-email source and no pod. clj/src/kakure/server.cljc remains their JVM reference runtime; nothing calls it."

  :adr/commits
  "gftdcojp/kakure 749d7c9 (the port), ef2e347 (deploy record). MIGRATION.edn carries the per-file dispositions."}]
